{
  "result": "Everyone has a junk drawer.\n\nNot the silverware drawer, not the one with the bills. The other one. The one that won't close all the way because there's a takeout menu folded over on itself and a single battery rolling around in there and a key that doesn't open anything in this house anymore, but you keep it anyway.\n\nWe all have a version of that drawer in our heads, too.\n\nMine is full of unfinished things. Half-remembered street names from vacations ten years ago. The exact way the light fell across my childhood kitchen floor at 4:32 p.m. in October. The feeling of being 19 on a bus, listening to a song for the first time and thinking, *oh, this will be important later* \u2014 and it wasn't, not really, but the feeling stuck.\n\nI think about how strange it is that we are the only animal that waves goodbye to places.\n\nWe do it without thinking. We check out of a hotel room and we look back once before closing the door. We drive away from an apartment we hated and still feel a lump in our throat at the last red light on that street. We stand at the gate at the airport after dropping someone off and watch until we can't see them anymore, even though we know they're just going to get coffee.\n\nWe say goodbye to countertops. To water stains on ceilings. To a particular crack in the sidewalk we learned to step over.\n\nMaybe that's because we don't live in places. We haunt them for a while.\n\nThe other day I was in a laundromat \u2014 one of the old ones with the big, humming fluorescent lights \u2014 and I watched an older man fold his shirts with a precision that made me feel like I should be quieter. Each shirt, same fold. Corners tucked. Stacked. He wasn't in a hurry. And I realized he probably had folded a shirt like that 20,000 times in his life. That's a kind of prayer, isn't it? Doing a small thing carefully for the 20,000th time when no one is watching.\n\nThat's what I want to write about when I'm told to write freely. Not big ideas. Small devotions.\n\nThe way people still look out the airplane window every time, even though it's just clouds. Every time. We act like we might see something new up there.\n\nThe way a dog has to run a full circle before it lies down, as if it's winding itself.\n\nThe way you can be having a completely normal Tuesday and a song from 2014 will come on in a grocery store and suddenly you are 24 again and standing in another grocery store in another city missing someone whose name you haven't said out loud in two years.\n\nNothing actually ends when it ends. It just gets filed. Into the drawer that doesn't close all the way.\n\nAnd somehow, we keep moving. We keep making new keys for new houses, knowing they'll end up in the drawer eventually too.",
